      <workList xml:id="workDesc_ee2e453b">
         <work xml:id="work_idc391325e">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_212bcf57">C4:003</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_id2479c400" xml:lang="de">Erich Pontoppidans Dänischer Atlas, oder Beschreibung des Königreichs Dännemark</title>
            <title type="original" xml:id="title_d08c2ead" xml:lang="da">Den danske Atlas, eller, Konge-Riget Dannemark</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_d39dddf6">
               <persName role="author" xml:id="persName_id65f7e94a">Erik Pontoppidan</persName>
               <persName role="translator" xml:id="persName_7e802143">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_idfd2084ba">
               <date enddate="1767" startdate="1765" xml:id="date_2bc7a7ca">1765–67</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_idd47c31dd">
               <p xml:id="idm159">In 1763 the first volumes of <persName xml:id="idm160" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Erik Pontoppidan</persName>’s large-scale, descriptive work on Denmark appeared, and two years later Scheibe’s translation was issued. As usual, Scheibe included a foreword regarding the translation, the revisions that he made to the original book, checking among other things Pontoppidan’s references, and the layout of the volumes. The translation excludes various descriptions included in the original Danish version which Scheibe thought would not be interesting for foreigners; however, instead he included other, more relevant descriptions borrowed from <persName xml:id="idm161" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Laurids de Thura</persName>’s <rend xml:id="idm162" fontstyle="italic">Haffnia hodierna</rend> (1748). Thus, according to Scheibe this was not merely a translation but indeed a revised and improved edition. It was apparently the author himself who already in the autumn of 1763 wished that Scheibe made the translation. Scheibe started working on the project a year later, but due to the death of Pontoppidan problems concerning the rights emerged. Scheibe finished his translation and revisions on both parts in August 1766 when he signed his foreword. In his pamphlet issued during the abolition of censorship in 1771 (SchW C3:001), Scheibe criticises Pontoppidan for not having been talented enough to successfully carry out the project: ‘Indeed, occasionally some [i.e. the Academy of Sciences or the Lord Chamberlain <persName xml:id="idm163" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Adam Gottlob Moltke</persName>] suggested a project in which he was not skilled and which he did not have insight enough to carry out, but merely to gain something from it’ (SchW C3:001; Danish, first edn., p. 39); and in an explanatory note Scheibe writes that ‘it is possible that the Danish Atlas may have its flaws and may perhaps not entirely deserve the costs used on it; but those who knew the venerable author have no reason to doubt that indeed the desire to work and to do himself and his fatherland honour was the sole motive for all his scholarly productions’ (Danish, first edn., p. 40).</p>
            </history>
